# Pointers to Structs in Go

Pointers to structs are a very common Go pattern, especially for structs with mutable state or large structs. Go provides **automatic dereference** that keeps the code clean.

## Creating Pointers to Structs

```go
type Person struct { Name string; Age int }

// Address of struct literal
p1 := &Person{Name: "Alice", Age: 30} // type: *Person

// Address of variable
person := Person{"Bob", 25}
p2 := &person

// Using new
p3 := new(Person)
p3.Name = "Charlie"
```

## Automatic Dereference

Go automatically dereferences pointers for field access:

```go
p := &Person{"Alice", 30}

// Verbose — not needed in Go
fmt.Println((*p).Name)

// Idiomatic — automatic dereference
fmt.Println(p.Name) // same result
p.Age++  // Go auto-does: (*p).Age++
```

## Passing Pointer to Function

```go
func applyDefaults(c *Config) {
    if c.Host == "" { c.Host = "localhost" }
    if c.Port == 0  { c.Port = 8080 }
}

cfg := Config{Debug: true}
applyDefaults(&cfg)
fmt.Println(cfg.Host, cfg.Port) // localhost 8080
```

## Comparing Pointers

```go
a := &Person{"Alice", 30}
b := a                    // same pointer
c := &Person{"Alice", 30} // different pointer, same value

fmt.Println(a == b)   // true
fmt.Println(a == c)   // false
fmt.Println(*a == *c) // true
```

